# Local junk
*.json
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.json
!tsconfig.*.json
!frontend/src/i18n/locales/en.json
!frontend/src/i18n/locales/ne.json
# reviewer notes for the Translation page (key -> where the sentence appears), read by translation_service
!translations/*_context.json
# English names + search words for the activity master list, read by scripts/seed_activity_search_terms.py
!backend/data/activity_search_terms.json
# H2.12: the supply-chain baselines are the gate. A blanket `*.json` above had
# already swallowed five files once (recovered in b7bf97d); a silently ignored
# baseline would instead make CI fail on every finding as though it were new.
!security/*.json

# ...and these are source too. The blanket `*.json` above is unanchored, so it
# matches every directory and silently swallows real config — `git add` reports
# nothing when it skips an ignored file. Already lost to it: the Sentry alert
# rules and dashboard, the Grafana dashboard, and .devcontainer/devcontainer.json,
# which a commit message claimed to add and did not. Anchoring the blanket to the
# repo root is NOT the fix: 4,049 of the untracked JSON files are backend caches
# (.mypy_cache, .pytest_cache, .ruff_cache) that should stay ignored.
!.devcontainer/*.json
!sentry/*.json
!load-tests/grafana-dashboards/*.json
!load-tests/grafana-datasources/*.json
!frontend/tsc-baseline.json
# H4.1: the three lint ratchets, for the same reason as tsc-baseline above.
# A silently ignored baseline is worse than none — CI would treat all 680
# known findings as new and go red on the first push. Fourth instance of
# this trap; each one was found by checking `git check-ignore` before the
# commit rather than after.
!backend/ruff-baseline.json
!backend/black-baseline.json
!frontend/eslint-baseline.json
# ...and the response-model gate's baseline (H4.8 / S3-47). SIXTH instance
# of this trap. A baseline that is green locally and ignored by git is a
# ratchet that only ever ratchets the author's machine.
!backend/response-model-baseline.json
!backend/scripts/unit_access_baseline.json
# ...and the security-rules gate's baseline (H4.6 / S2-42 / S3-09). SEVENTH
# instance of the same trap. Without the exception, CI would treat all 733
# known violations as new on the first push and go red.
!frontend/security-rules-baseline.json
# ...and the any-count gate's baseline (H4.5 / S2-24). EIGHTH instance of the
# same trap. Without it, the `: any` ratchet would be green locally, ignored
# by git, and red on the first push.
!frontend/any-count-baseline.json
# ...and the perf gate's baseline, swallowed by the same rule. FIFTH
# instance. CI's `npm run perf:validate` failed with "Missing baseline"
# while it passed locally, because the file exists here and was never
# published. That is the whole failure mode of this trap: a gate that is
# green on the machine that wrote it and red everywhere else.
!frontend/.lighthouse-baseline.json
# Test fixtures are source, not build output.  The blanket `*.json` above was
# silently swallowing them: `git add <dir>` skips ignored files WITHOUT an
# error, so committed test files referenced fixtures that were never in the
# repo and would fail on a fresh clone.
!tests/fixtures/*.json
!**/__tests__/fixtures/*.json
# The DBH class-key contract artefact, and it needs its own line: the two above
# do not cover it. A pattern containing a slash is anchored to this file's own
# directory, so `tests/fixtures/*.json` means `<root>/tests/fixtures/` and not
# `backend/tests/fixtures/`; only the `**/` prefix on the second one reaches
# anywhere. It is a fixture by the definition given above -- the database holds
# the keys and bounds, this file lets the frontend's label join see them, and
# `backend/tests/test_dbh_class_keys_fixture.py` holds it to the live table. Left
# uncommitted it would fail on a fresh clone in exactly the way the comment
# above warns about, and silently, because `git add` skips ignored files.
!backend/tests/fixtures/*.json
!.pre-commit-config.yaml
!.secrets.baseline
!Makefile
!docker-compose.yml
!docker-compose.*.yml

